{
  "dom_id": "step-q161866",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14156477/assets/675226780bb9627524f4ab03691e84e312300f398f352abc3b698c1fcdd83490.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-1$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\nI. $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=0$\n\nII. ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=1$\n\nIII. The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=0$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)}$",
